1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:03,288
Over the next three years, Haber kept pushing —

2
00:00:03,288 --> 00:00:05,024
and he didn't do it alone.

3
00:00:05,024 --> 00:00:07,361
His assistant, Robert Le Rossignol,

4
00:00:07,361 --> 00:00:12,102
was the one who actually designed and built the high-pressure apparatus

5
00:00:12,102 --> 00:00:14,239
that made the breakthrough real.

6
00:00:14,239 --> 00:00:15,975
By 1908, working together,

7
00:00:15,975 --> 00:00:21,250
they had a tabletop device that could hold roughly 200 atmospheres of pressure,

8
00:00:21,250 --> 00:00:26,659
using an osmium-and-uranium catalyst, sustaining a far more usable reaction rate.

9
00:00:26,659 --> 00:00:27,794
In 1908 and 1909,

10
00:00:27,794 --> 00:00:32,401
Haber filed the patent with Le Rossignol's name on it beside his own.

11
00:00:32,401 --> 00:00:34,338
It was a laboratory triumph —

12
00:00:34,338 --> 00:00:37,342
and still a world away from feeding a planet.

